    <token name="@TOOL_VERSION@">2.0.9</token>
    <token name="@VERSION_SUFFIX@">0</token>
    <token name="@PROFILE@">26.0</token>
    <!--
        CONTAINER-ONLY tool (NOT on bioconda). The upstream hillerlab/TOGA2
        repo ships ONLY an apptainer/Singularity def
        (supply/containers/apptainer.def); there is no published Docker image,
        no bioconda package and no biocontainer, so this tool cannot resolve
        through conda like the rest of this repo.

        `tools/toga2/container/` fills that gap: it builds a pullable OCI
        image, so this token is a `docker://` reference the container resolver
        fetches like any other. A deployment no longer has to build a 5.5 GB
        SIF and stage it on shared storage before this tool can run at all.
        The def stays in this directory: it remains the way to produce a SIF
        for a site that wants one.

        &#8859; THE IMAGE IS NOT A REPRODUCTION OF THE DEF. It builds from
        upstream's OWN conda.yaml and requirements.txt, which the def does not
        use &#8212; the def takes whatever python continuumio/anaconda3 carries
        and installs Nextflow from get.nextflow.io at build time. See the
        README beside the Dockerfile for every deviation and why.

        &#9888; PINNED TO v2.0.9, AND THE MOVE OFF v2.0.8 WAS A BUG FIX, NOT
        HOUSEKEEPING. Through v2.0.8, TOGA2's own
        nextflow_configs/execute_joblist.nf put its argument guard and channel
        construction at the top level, which Nextflow's strict parser (25.10
        and later) rejects outright: "Statements cannot be mixed with script
        declarations". v2.0.9 moved both inside `workflow {}`. Measured across
        both Nextflow generations, v2.0.6/7a/8 fail on 26.04.6 and pass on
        24.10.5, while v2.0.9 passes on both. That is why the command block no
        longer exports NXF_SYNTAX_PARSER=v1, and why pinning Nextflow backwards
        is the wrong response should this recur &#8212; check whether upstream
        fixed the script first.

        &#9888; NOTE THE LAYOUT MOVED TOO: v2.0.9 relocated conda.yaml to
        assets/venv/ and requirements.txt to assets/requirements/. The
        Dockerfile discovers both rather than hardcoding either.

        &#9888; THIS IS A DIGEST, NOT A TAG, ON PURPOSE. `:2.0.9` is whatever
        was pushed there last; the digest below is the exact image whose build
        ran a complete TOGA2 projection and produced the four-file output
        schema this wrapper declares. A job can therefore say afterwards which
        image produced its result &#8212; and "the container was silently
        substituted" is a failure this project has already been bitten by.
        The equivalent tag is `quay.io/galaxy/toga2:2.0.9`; if you re-pin to a
        rebuilt image, re-run the WF-C.4 comparison first.

        Deployments that keep the image elsewhere only need to override this
        token.
    -->
    <token name="@TOGA_IMAGE@">docker://quay.io/galaxy/toga2@sha256:2c30f162d9b026406f5f975492d40dd1e15cf8369014cf4b3ee731662f66fbe5</token>
    <xml name="requirements">
        <requirements>
            <container type="singularity">@TOGA_IMAGE@</container>
        </requirements>
    </xml>
